      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the minimum value of the expression\n\\[\n\\frac{a}{2b} + \\frac{b}{4c} + \\frac{c}{8a},\n\\]\nwe can utilize the AM-GM (Arithmetic Mean-Geometric Mean) inequality. The AM-GM inequality states that for any non-negative real numbers \\(x, y, z\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{x + y + z}{3} \\geq \\sqrt[3]{xyz}.\n\\]\nEquality holds if and only if \\(x = y = z\\).\n\nFirst, let's apply the AM-GM inequality to the three terms in the given expression:\n\\[\n\\frac{a}{2b} + \\frac{b}{4c} + \\frac{c}{8a}.\n\\]\nTo apply AM-GM, we need to combine these terms appropriately. Notice that we can rewrite the expression by introducing common factors:\n\\[\n\\frac{a}{2b} = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{a}{b}, \\quad \\frac{b}{4c} = \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{b}{c}, \\quad \\frac{c}{8a} = \\frac{1}{8} \\cdot \\frac{c}{a}.\n\\]\nThus, the expression becomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{a}{b} + \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{b}{c} + \\frac{1}{8} \\cdot \\frac{c}{a}.\n\\]\nFor simplicity, let's set:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{a}{b}, \\quad y = \\frac{b}{c}, \\quad z = \\frac{c}{a}.\n\\]\nNotice that \\(x, y, z\\) are positive real numbers because \\(a, b, c > 0\\). The AM-GM inequality then simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\frac{x + y + z}{3} \\geq \\sqrt[3]{xyz}.\n\\]\nSince \\(x, y, z\\) are positive, we have:\n\\[\nxyz = \\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right) \\left(\\frac{b}{c}\\right) \\left(\\frac{c}{a}\\right) = 1.\n\\]\nTherefore,\n\\[\n\\sqrt[3]{xyz} = \\sqrt[3]{1} = 1.\n\\]\nSo,\n\\[\n\\frac{x + y + z}{3} \\geq 1.\n\\]\nMultiplying both sides by 3, we get:\n\\[\nx + y + z \\geq 3.\n\\]\nHence,\n\\[\n\\frac{a}{2b} + \\frac{b}{4c} + \\frac{c}{8a} \\geq 3.\n\\]\nTo check if this minimum value can be achieved, we need to see if equality holds in the AM-GM inequality, which occurs when \\(x = y = z\\). Therefore, we need:\n\\[\n\\frac{a}{b} = \\frac{b}{c} = \\frac{c}{a}.\n\\]\nLet \\(k\\) be this common ratio. Then,\n\\[\na = kb, \\quad b = kc, \\quad c = ka.\n\\]\nFrom \\(a = kb\\) and \\(b = kc\\), we get \\(a = k^2c\\). From \\(c = ka\\), we get \\(c = ka\\). Substituting \\(a = k^2c\\) into \\(c = ka\\), we get \\(c = k^2c\\), hence \\(k^2 = 1\\), so \\(k = 1\\) (since \\(k\\) is positive).\n\nSubstituting \\(k = 1\\) back, we verify that:\n\\[\na = b = c.\n\\]\nThus, the minimum value of the sum is indeed achieved when \\(a = b = c\\), and the minimum value is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}.\n\\]",
